# contingent: S3 links "possible not to be" to "at some time is not." That is a further premise about the same modal claim, not a second sense of it.
# The well-known weakness at C1 (S4) is a quantifier shift: from each thing failing to exist at some time, to a time when nothing exists. That is not a shift in what any claim means.
# necessary: S7 and S8–S10 both seem to mean existence that cannot fail, i.e. not generable or corruptible. Necessity caused by another is introduced as a subdivision of that one sense, not as a different sense.
